From f456414ee155d0e5f1dd533fcbf341ad9c5e371a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Trung=20L=C3=AA?= <8@tle.id.au>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 09:40:45 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 15/30] Add a VSX backend for PowerPC to jxl_simd

jxl-rs has SIMD backends for x86-64, AArch64 and wasm32 only, so JPEG XL
decoding on PowerPC runs the scalar path. Carry the VSX backend from the
jxl-rs vsx-simd branch in the in-tree jxl_simd, which is already path-sourced
for the macOS x86-64 workaround.

The backend is modelled on Highway's ppc_vsx-inl.h and needs POWER8 VSX at
compile time, which is the powerpc64le baseline. A big-endian build only gets
it when Rust targets POWER8 or later (e.g. -C target-cpu=pwr9), so the
PowerMac G4/G5 keep the scalar path. On little-endian, POWER9 is detected at
runtime, and the f16 conversions then use the hardware instructions.

It is enabled by a vsx feature that image/rust/jxl turns on for powerpc64.
The PowerPC intrinsics are still unstable, so jxl_simd joins the crates
allowed RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP on PowerPC; the feature gates are confined to it.

Decode speed with jxl 0.7.4, one thread, POWER9 (scalar -> VSX):
  ppc64le: bike 4.2 -> 8.4 MP/s, progressive_ac 7.7 -> 11.7 MP/s
Output differs from the scalar path by at most one level in under 0.005% of
bytes, from rounding.
